license: MIT
version: "1.0.0"
abstract: >
  A live-model, hash-manifested empirical study of four tool-agent failure
  families (CONFUSED_DEPUTY, DESTRUCTIVE_WRITE, EXFILTRATION,
  UNTRUSTED_TO_ACTION), comparing a packaged baseline guardrail against
  four family-specific structured guardrails on a frozen 66-candidate
  corpus. Findings include confirmed genuine security improvements,
  confirmed regressions (a specialized guardrail measurably worse than the
  naive baseline), matched-control false-allow/false-deny rates, and
  component-level ablation results attributing each gain to its specific
  mechanism. This work is a research extension of, and depends on the
  SDK/test environment (MIT-licensed) published by, the "AI Agent Security:
  Multi-Step Tool Attacks" Kaggle competition (OpenAI, Google, and IEEE,
  2026) -- see references below.
